{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is historically accurate. In the United States, Black people were explicitly denied voting rights because of race through laws restricting voting to white people.\n\nThe Fifteenth Amendment, ratified in 1870, prohibited racial discrimination in voting, but many states subsequently used literacy tests, poll taxes, discriminatory registration practices, and intimidation to disenfranchise Black voters. Some measures were race-neutral in wording but deliberately designed or administered to exclude Black people. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 targeted these practices.\n\nThe claim also applies elsewhere—for example, apartheid South Africa excluded Black people from the national franchise. It does not mean every Black person, everywhere, has always been denied voting rights."}}
